riscos-mesa: the Raspberry Pi's hello_pi examples, rebuilt
==========================================================

These are three of the Raspberry Pi's own hello_pi examples, rebuilt from
source against riscos-mesa (EGL, OpenGL ES and the DispmanX compatibility
library). They show that existing Pi Khronos programs port to riscos-mesa.
They run on any RISC OS 5 machine, the Pi 4 included.

Needs SharedUnixLibrary and ARMEABISupport (the !Run files load them from
System:Modules if they aren't loaded already).

On the Pi these programs take over the whole screen. Here riscos-mesa's
DispmanX library shows each one's "display" in a desktop window instead,
640 pixels wide, so they multitask. Close the window to quit. Each !Run
file has a line to choose another size, or full screen as on the Pi (then
they don't multitask, and a key or Menu click quits).

!HelloTriangle   spinning cube with three photo textures (OpenGL ES 1.1).
                 Close the window (or press any key) to quit.
!HelloTriangle2  Julia set over a Mandelbrot set (OpenGL ES 2.0 shaders).
                 Move the mouse to change it; close the window or click
                 Menu to quit.
                 The shaders run on the CPU, so it renders about 320
                 pixels wide and scales that up to its window.
                 *Set HelloTriangle2$Scale N renders at 1/N of the screen
                 size instead (lower N: sharper and slower).
!HelloTeapot     a lit, textured teapot model (OpenGL ES 1.1). On the Pi
                 it played a video on the teapot through the GPU's video
                 decoder; there is none here, so it wears a still picture.
                 Close the window (or press any key) to quit.

Please report: whether they multitask in their windows, whether the
textures show on the cube, whether the fractals
follow the mouse, whether the teapot looks right, and roughly how many
frames a second each manages.

The sources are in ports/hello_pi in the riscos-mesa repository, and the
guide to porting other Pi programs is docs/porting/hello_pi.md.
